@page "/learn/validatable-type-stable"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Nested validation · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Nested validation</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your form checks the company name. Does it check the contacts?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Forms · Static SSR · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>[ValidatableType]</code>, the attribute that makes a form check the
        objects inside its model, is now a supported API. In .NET 10 the same thing worked, but the attribute was
        experimental, and the way the docs showed it didn't build. This page shows what the attribute does, then
        what changed.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>New supplier</i> form. A supplier has a company name, an address and a list of contacts. Every
        field is <code>[Required]</code>, and the emails are <code>[EmailAddress]</code> too.
    </p>
    <p>Here's the model, with no attribute on it:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The model: nothing marks it</span>
        <pre class="snip">public sealed class SupplierDraft : ISupplierForm
{
    [Required, StringLength(40)] public string? Company { get; set; }
    <span class="err">public AddressForm Address { get; set =&gt; field = value ?? new(); } = new();</span>
    <span class="err">public List&lt;ContactForm&gt; Contacts { get; set =&gt; field = value ?? []; } = [new(), new()];</span>
}</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The form starts with three mistakes: the city is empty, Ann's email is <code>sales@@</code>, and Bo has no
        email. Try to save it.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The form, without the attribute" Url="demo/nested-validation/without"
                Label="Without [ValidatableType]" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Save supplier</b>. It saves: the empty city, the bad email and the missing one. The green
            banner shows what went in.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Only the top level is checked</h2>
    <p>The form looks flat, but the model behind it has layers:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The model the form posts</span>
        <pre class="snip">SupplierDraft
├─ Company    <span class="dim">[Required]</span>            <span class="ok">✓ checked</span>
├─ Address
│  └─ City    <span class="dim">[Required]</span>            <span class="err">never checked</span>
└─ Contacts   <span class="dim">List&lt;ContactForm&gt;</span>
   └─ [0], [1]: Name, Email <span class="dim">[Required, EmailAddress]</span>  <span class="err">never checked</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;</code> checks the rules on the model's own properties, and stops there.
        <code>Address</code> and <code>Contacts</code> have no rules of their own, so it never looks inside them. The
        only rule it runs is on <code>Company</code>, and that passes.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Mark the model</h2>
    <p>
        Put <code>[ValidatableType]</code> on the form's model, and register validation once in <code>Program.cs</code>.
        In .NET 11 this builds. In .NET 10 the same <code>using</code> line broke the build (more below).
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix, in .NET 11</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">using Microsoft.Extensions.Validation;</span>

<span class="hl">[ValidatableType]</span>
public sealed class NewSupplierForm { ... }

<span class="dim">// Program.cs</span>
<span class="hl">builder.Services.AddValidation();</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        At compile time, .NET reads the marked type and follows its properties: <code>Address</code> to
        <code>AddressForm</code>, and <code>Contacts</code> to each <code>ContactForm</code>. It generates validation
        code for all three, so every level gets checked. Here's the same form, same mistakes, with the mark.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same form, marked" Url="demo/nested-validation/with"
                Label="With [ValidatableType]">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Save supplier</b>. <b>City</b> and both emails are flagged, and nothing saves. Press
            <b>+ Add contact</b> and save again: the new empty row is flagged too.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>The two demos differ by one attribute. Both models live in this file:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/NestedValidation/SupplierForms.cs" Feature="validatable-type-stable">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1 posts <code>SupplierDraft</code>. Fig. 2 posts <code>NewSupplierForm</code>, the marked one. They
            share an interface, not a base class. If <code>SupplierDraft</code> were the base class, marking
            <code>NewSupplierForm</code> would get it checked too, and Fig. 1 wouldn't fail.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>An experimental attribute</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 10 the official attribute was experimental. The SDK also added a hidden copy of it to each Blazor
        project. How you wrote it decided which one you got:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to write it in .NET 10, and what the build said</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">[ValidatableType]</pre>
                <span class="why">uses the hidden copy the SDK adds to your project</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ builds and validates</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">using Microsoft.Extensions.Validation;</span>
[ValidatableType]</pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ CS0104: ambiguous between the hidden copy and the public attribute</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="err">#pragma warning disable ASP0029</span>
[Microsoft.Extensions.Validation.ValidatableType]
<span class="err">#pragma warning restore ASP0029</span></pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ without the pragma, error ASP0029: "for evaluation purposes only and is subject to change or removal in future updates"</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The bare attribute worked, but it wasn't the one the docs described, and the official one needed a
        <code>#pragma</code> to build. Nothing about it said "safe to rely on".
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>One supported attribute</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 there's no hidden copy and no experimental error. <code>[ValidatableType]</code> is a supported API in
        <code>Microsoft.Extensions.Validation</code>. Add the <code>using</code>, mark the model, and it builds. That's
        what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Only the top model needs the attribute.</b> The types inside it are found through its properties.</li>
        <li><b>Marking a class turns checks on for its base class too,</b> wherever that base class is used, even where nothing marks it.</li>
        <li><b>Forget <code>AddValidation()</code> and only the top level is checked.</b> <code>DataAnnotationsValidator</code> won't look inside it.</li>
        <li><b>The mark only works in the project that calls <code>AddValidation()</code>.</b> Mark a model in a shared class library and nothing gets checked. .NET 11 warns you about this with ASP0038.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
